# Security Rules — CodeJudgeX

## Authentication

- JWT access tokens: short-lived (15 min default), HMAC-SHA256, secret from env only
- Refresh tokens: long-lived, stored hashed in PostgreSQL, rotated on every use
- BCrypt strength 12 minimum for all password hashing
- Never log, print, or return password hashes or refresh token hashes
- Logout must invalidate the refresh token in PostgreSQL

## Authorization

- Every protected Spring Boot endpoint annotated with `@PreAuthorize` or role check in service
- Role hierarchy: STUDENT < FACULTY < ADMIN < SUPER_ADMIN
- Hidden test cases: service layer must strip them before any student-facing response
- Source code: students may only fetch their own submissions — enforce in service, not just controller
- Admin endpoints: never accessible to STUDENT or FACULTY — enforce with `@PreAuthorize("hasRole('ADMIN')")`

## Data protection

- Never expose: `passwordHash`, `refreshTokenHash`, hidden test case `inputData`/`expectedOutput` to students, internal stack traces in error responses
- Sensitive fields must be excluded from MapStruct response DTO mappings
- No credentials in logs — mask any field named `password`, `token`, `secret`, `key`

## API security

- CORS: explicit allowlist only — never `*` in production
- Rate limiting on: `/auth/login`, `/auth/register`, `/submissions` (POST), `/auth/refresh`
- Parameterized queries always — Spring Data JPA + JPQL handles this; never string-concatenate into queries
- Input validation at every controller boundary — reject before any business logic runs
- HTTPS enforced in production — never transmit tokens over HTTP

## Infrastructure

- All secrets via environment variables — never hardcoded, never in `application.yml` literals
- `infra/.env` is gitignored — only `infra/.env.example` is committed
- Judge0 CE executes user code in a sandbox — Spring Boot must never `Runtime.exec()` or `ProcessBuilder` user-supplied code
- Rotate any secret immediately if it touches a committed file — then audit git history

## Incident rule

Every security incident: write a postmortem entry in `.obsidian-ai-memory/03-ERRORS/error-memory.md`
and promote the prevention rule to `.obsidian-ai-memory/03-ERRORS/anti-patterns.md`.
